        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I was thinking if it would be possible to access the winbar remaining after having removed the buffers. In that sense, I was thinking about an option of creating a dummy file when no more buffers are left on my window layout.\r\n\r\nThere is always some buffer present as long as Neovim is showing some window. As `:h windows-intro` says: \"A window is a viewport on a buffer.\". So as long as there is window ('winbar' of which you want to customize), there is a buffer.\r\n\r\nSo yes, I stand corrected that 'mini.bufremove' does create a buffer if user wants to unshow the last buffer.\r\nI don't think adding some special filetype to it is a good design, because the whole idea behind that buffer is to be a generic scratch buffer.\r\n\r\n> What I was trying to achieve is to customize the window bar after all buffers are removed. I would need a way of explicitly get an event on winenter/bufenter when all the buffers are removed, a way of setting a pattern on that event for that concrete window\r\n\r\nI would suggest one of at least these choices:\r\n- Make your 'winbar' work on scratch buffers (ones with `vim.bo.buftype` equal to `'nofile'`).\r\n- Make your 'winbar' explicitly count number of open (and possibly listed) buffers. See `:h nvim_list_bufs()` and `:h buflisted()`.\r\n- Use `BufEnter` or `BufWinEnter` event to compute that number of buffers.\r\n\r\nSo with this in mind, unfortunately I don't think this suggestion will end up in 'mini.bufremove'.",
            "created_at": "2023-06-16T17:20:33Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/372#issuecomment-1595006992",
